  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sterngthening Enforcement Against the Illegal Pangolin and Hippopotamus Trafficking in Uganda</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project will counter wildlife trafficking of key species in Uganda including pangolins, hippopotamus, and okapi. Pangolins are poached for their scales (to supply the demand in Asia) and meat, which is offered in many Chinese-owned restaurants serving Ugandas growing Asian population. Hippopotamus teeth or ivory are sought as a less expensive, lower risk alternative to elephant ivory. Okapis are locally extinct in Uganda but are increasingly found in seizures, indicating a transboundary trade between Uganda and the Democratic Republic of Congo. The Natural Resource Conservation Network is the only non-governmental entity in Uganda with the mandate to prosecute wildlife crimes. This two-year grant will support: a data entry officer to manage historic and ongoing wildlife crime data; development of an improved data collection and management system, including a system to map criminal networks; a data analyst to compile and analyze data on wildlife trafficking networks; targeted field investigations and prosecutions of wildlife traffickers; coalition-building and information sharing with local and international enforcement authorities to address transboundary trafficking networks; secure storage of confidential information related to wildlife cases; workshops with relevant authorities on changes to wildlife laws; and media outreach to raise awareness of the impacts of wildlife trafficking.</narrative>
  </description>
